<title>BELGIUM: Belgian beet test shows yield down, density up.</title>
<headline>Belgian beet test shows yield down, density up.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-08-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The first Belgian beet test of the year showed sugar yields per root lower than in 1996, but sugar per hectare higher due to denser plantings.  </p>
<p>The Royal Belgian Sugar Beet Institute said root weight was 39,669 kg per hectare , with a sugar yield of 13.25 percent, compared with 30,971 kg/ha and 15.94 percent a year earlier.</p>
<p>The average for the 1987 to 1996 period was 36,548 kg/ha with a yield of 13.76 percent.</p>
<p>Sugar yield per hectare was 5,256 kg/ha versus 4,936 kg/ha in 1996 and an average in the last 10 years of 5,027 kg/ha.</p>
<p>The test was carried out on August 4.</p>
<p>--Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6810, Fax +32 2 230 7710</p>
